export const ANCIENT_CHAPTER_26_CONTENT = `
# Chapter 26: From Ancient to Medieval

## 📉 Decline of Trade and Rise of Feudalism
The period following the decline of the Gupta Empire (c. 500 CE) marks the transition from Ancient to early Medieval India. 
- **Urban Decay:** Long-distance trade (especially with the Roman Empire) collapsed. Towns decayed, and metallic money (coins) became noticeably scarce.
- **Closed Economy:** The economy transformed from a dynamic, cash-based commercial system to a **closed, agrarian, self-sufficient village economy** (Jajmani system).

## 📜 Land Grants and Landed Intermediaries
To adapt to the lack of cash revenues, the state resorted to paying priests and officers through land.
- **Brahmadeya / Agrahara:** Land granted to Brahmins (often tax-free and in perpetuity). 
- **Sub-infeudation:** The king handed over his rights to collect taxes, administer justice, and control the police to the land grantee. 
- **Serfdom:** The peasants (cultivators) were transferred along with the land. They lost mobility, were subjected to forced labor (**Vishti/Begar**), and became tied to the landlord, creating a system of **Indian Feudalism (Samanta system)**.

## 👥 Social Fragmentation: Proliferation of Jatis
The theoretical four-varna system shattered into thousands of castes (Jatis).
- **Assimilating Tribes:** As Brahmins expanded agriculture into forests via land grants, aboriginal tribes were absorbed. Chiefs became 'Rajputs/Kshatriyas', while ordinary tribesmen became 'Shudras/Untouchables'.
- **New Classes:** The Kayasthas (scribes) emerged as a powerful new caste to manage complex land records. 
- **Shudras vs. Vaishyas:** As trade declined, Vaishyas lost status. Shudras transitioned from being slaves/servants to becoming the main peasant/agricultural class.
- **Untouchability:** Peaked in severity, with some castes deemed 'unseeable'.

## 👩 Position of Women
Society became deeply patriarchal and conservative.
- Marriage age was lowered (child marriage).
- Rights were severely curtailed. Women essentially had no right to inherit real estate (property), possessing only movable **Stridhana** (gifts/jewelry).
- Practices like **Sati** and the **Devadasi** (temple women) system became institutionalized.

## 🛕 Emergence of Regional Cultures & Religion
- **Political Fragmentation:** The centralized empire was replaced by constantly warring regional kingdoms (Rajputs, Palas, Pratiharas, Rashtrakutas).
- **Language & Art:** Pan-Indian Sanskrit fragmented into regional Apabhramshas (Proto-Hindi, Bengali, Marathi). Distinct regional temple architecture styles rapidly developed.
- **Bhakti & Tantra:** Vedic sacrifices died out. Religion became centered on massive **Temple complexes** (puja) and **Bhakti** (personal devotion to Vishnu or Shiva). **Tantrism**, absorbing tribal goddess fertility cults, deeply influenced all religious practices.
- **The Temple Economy:** The temple itself functioned as a massive feudal landlord, bank, and center of political legitimation.
`;

